Database(MySQL, Postgres, oracle) Vs Data Warehouse(teradata, exadata, redshift, snowflake)

Data warehouse is built on top of database(made from multiple databases) but used for different purposes. 
DW handles larges amounts of data, has read-heavy operations, optimal for high-latency operations, 
contains denormalized data(can contain redundant data), columnar storage(parquet), optimized for parallel processing, 
OLAP systems use data warehousing solutions. DW solutions work mostly on structured data(as opposed to Spark). 
DW integrates data coming from multiple sources.

Database is designed to store small amounts of data, for write-heavy operations, used for low-latency operations, normalized data, row-based storage, 
not optimized for parallel processing, OLTP use database solutions

In DW, we do ETL(Extract data from sources, transform the data and load into data warehouse/databases). 
Problem with this is - the rate at which big data is generated(3V's) this can't transform it with that speed and with this we also have to know 
the transformations that have to be applied to data beforehand(requires pre-defined schema), can handle only structured data, 
and is expensive as data processing and storage needs increase and due to vertical scaling it can be scaled only to a certain point. 
This is where data lake comes into picture.

Data lake(s3 bucket, azure blob, HDFS) - lets you load all of the data into data storage and transform it on need basis; 
unlimited storage due to horizontal scaling. It supports structured, semi-struc, unstructured data.

In intial data lake systems, you could just use it for data storage and then pass it to data warehouse for transformation. 
But now data lake systems have become refined and they can handle transformations as well. 
They consist of 4 layers - 
Ingestion layer - raw data storage 
Processing - process the data 
Processed - processed data based on use case(engineer level data access) 
Consumption - business users/reporting tools take data from this layer(business users data access). At this stage, data could also be sent to a 
database/data warehouse(optional)

Data Mart - can be considered a subset of data warehouse containing data for a specific purpose. 
Data from different departments/demographics/regions is fetched from DW and put into data marts through ETL. 
Useful for business users who want only relevant data, or summarized tables and enables faster query performance. 
It also protects data about different departments or other important data from unwanted access.

Data from source system goes to DW through ETL and then to a BI system for generating reports

Extract:

DW has two layers - staging layer and user access layer
Staging layer is like the landing zone for data coming from source systems but to send data to 
user access layer we'll have to apply dimensional modeling to transform the data in the right formats.

Types of staging layer - 
Persistent(the data coming from source system is preserved) and Non-persistent layer(once the data moves to user access layer the data is removed from staging layer)

Transform: Uniformity & Restructuring 
Common Transformations - 
  Data Value Unification(values for categorical columns in source and destination systems should be same; eg-   No/N/0)

  Data type and size uniformity - same data type and size of column (eg- varchar(32))
  De-duplication - if the same data already exists in master table then not adding it again
  Dropping Columns - removing unnecessary cols
  Dropping records

Load:

Initial load & Incremental Load

Initial Load - done only once before DW goes live
Incremental Load - To keep DW updated (data about new customers, modified data, delete data)
Types of incremental load: append, in-place update, complete replacement, rolling append(maintaining data for a given time period on a rolling basis; eg- weekly data updates to maintain 24 month data)

Fact and Dimension Tables -

Fact table: some kind of measurement(eg- trnx, sales, orders data); stores measurable data usualyy numbers and connects to dimensions table for context info
Dimension table: adds context to data in fact table(eg - customers, time, product, stores data)
Fact tables will have foreign keys to PK in dimension tables to add context

How to build fact table?

Understanding business context, needs, the analysis they want and the data they have
grain/granularity - level of details present in the fact table; more granularity is important so that the tables can answer questions but it also increases storage used

Identify the dimensions - Based on granularity, we can decide on different dimensions

Identifying the facts - based on grains

Derived fact - columns derived from other columns; increases data size; Better to create views to see data with derived columns

Types of fact in fact table:
additivity - to add

Three types of facts: 
additive(can add fact/column along any dimension)  - 
Facts (numbers) that can be added across all dimensions (like time, product, store).
🍭 Example:
You sold 100 candies on Monday, 200 on Tuesday → You sold 300 total.
🟢 Use: Sales revenue, quantity sold, profit.

semi-additive(can add along some dimension) -
You can add these across some dimensions (like product or store) but not time.
🍭 Example:
If you have 50 candies in stock today, and 50 tomorrow, you don’t have 100 total – it’s still 50 at a point in time.
🟠 Use: Inventory, account balance, daily stock.

non-additive(cannot add along any dimension) -

You can’t add it at all.
🍭 Example:
You calculate a customer satisfaction score. You can’t just add those scores across days or customers.
🔴 Use: Ratios, percentages, rates (e.g., conversion rate, average score).




Types of fact table:
Transaction fact table - stores records at the most granualar level with one record per trxn; each record gives details of transaction; 1 record per trnx// data captured at PoS

Periodic Snaposhot fact table - Captures data at regular intervals (daily, monthly) to track change over time. Example: Daily balance of a bank account, monthly subscriptions. Usually semi-additive: You can sum across accounts but not time.



Accumulating Snapshot fact table - Tracks the lifecycle of a process (start → intermediate → end).
Example: Order fulfillment – order placed, packed, shipped, delivered. Useful for processes with definable steps.

Factless fact table - Tracks events without numeric/measurable data, just foreign keys to dimension tables.
doesn't contain any fact columns; cols linking different dimensions to add context and find relationships(eg: table containing dateId, prodId, storeId, promoId)
Example: To track which customers signed up for promotions,

Aggregate Fact table - Stores pre-aggregated data to improve performance. Used to speed up reporting when raw data is too large. Example: Total daily sales by product category.


